      "rows": [
        {
          "row_id": "r0",
          "label": "Business description — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "Designer, producer and supplier of engineered aircraft components for commercial and military platforms. K page:4: “designer, producer and supplier of highly engineered aircraft components”. Product groups include power controls, actuators, pumps, valves, sensors, latches, cabin/security equipment, restraints and testing systems; K pages 5–6. Management's “leading” characterization is not adopted.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C251",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Design-in positions link component demand to aircraft production and subsequent maintenance, rather than a single initial sale.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C251",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Evaluate installed-base demand separately from new-build cyclicality; component breadth alone does not prove independence of end-market shocks.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C251",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r1",
          "label": "Revenue streams and durability — Management Language",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:4: “Once our parts are designed into and sold on a new aircraft, we generate net sales from aftermarket consumption over the life of that aircraft”; “Historically, these aftermarket revenues have produced a higher gross profit and have been more stable than net sales to original equipment manufacturers”.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C252",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Repeated replacement/maintenance demand can extend revenue beyond the initial aircraft sale; historical management characterization is not a guarantee or a contracted annuity.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C252",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Supports a revenue-durability hypothesis, but debt service remains exposed to fleet utilization and airline economics. Aftermarket mix measure: reference CP-0, Located financial, operating and transaction coverage / Business and operating model; no new numeric estimate adopted here.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C252",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r2",
          "label": "Segment operating model — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:5: “Power & Control, Airframe and Non-aviation.” K page:6 identifies original-equipment and aftermarket channels for the aviation segments. Segment sales and earnings measures: reference CP-1, Earnings definitions, add-backs and segment binding, bound to K pages 49–50.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C253",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Different component functions broaden product exposure, but common aviation customers and platforms transmit correlated demand shocks.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C253",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Use CP-1's segment binding; do not add corporate reconciliation as a revenue segment or assume product breadth eliminates concentration.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C253",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r3",
          "label": "Customer channels and geography — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:9: “distributors of aerospace components”; “worldwide commercial airlines”; “various armed forces of the United States and friendly foreign governments”. OEMs, system suppliers and industrial buyers are also listed. K page:8 names Boeing Distribution Services and Satair among major distributors.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C254",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Exposure includes distributors and OEM supply chains as well as end users; geographic reach introduces procurement, export and customer-payment dependencies.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C254",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Monitor end-user/platform exposure behind distributor sales. Geographic sales split and customer-level receivables exposure: Not supplied in this fact pack; no geographic percentage inferred.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C254",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r4",
          "label": "Commercial volume drivers — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:9: aftermarket demand depends on “the breadth of our installed OEM base, revenue passenger kilometers”; fleet size/age, warranty status and airline profitability are also specified. K page:11: “Our commercial transport OEM shipments and revenues generally run ahead of aircraft delivery schedules.”",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C255",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Utilization supports aftermarket consumption; OEM scheduling and supply constraints can change shipments before aircraft deliveries and can distort short-period comparisons.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C255",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Stress utilization and OEM builds separately; order timing is not contracted revenue visibility. Unit volumes, backlog coverage and cancellation-adjusted order book measures: Not supplied; not zero.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C255",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r5",
          "label": "Defense exposure — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:9: “The demand for defense products is specifically dependent on government budget trends, military campaigns and political pressures.” K page:11 describes spending timing and reprioritization uncertainty.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C256",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Budgets and procurement timing can interrupt orders even when operational demand persists.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C256",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Do not treat defense exposure as wholly countercyclical or guaranteed; monitor funding and order timing alongside commercial demand.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C256",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r6",
          "label": "Pricing and cost discipline — Management Language",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:5: “carefully controlling the cost structure via productivity and cost improvements and pricing our highly engineered value-added products to fairly reflect the value we provide”. K page:8 locates engineering expense in cost of sales and S&A, and R&D in S&A.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C257",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Management seeks value-based pricing and productivity; input-cost inflation, engineering spend and certification obligations can offset realization.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C257",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Margin resilience requires actual price/cost and productivity evidence, not the strategy statement. Price/volume bridge and contractual inflation pass-through measure: Not supplied; status Not disclosed in the inspected evidence.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C257",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r7",
          "label": "Cost base and capital spending — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:8: “Most of our manufacturing facilities are comprised of manufacturing, distribution and engineering functions”; improvement actions include automation, tooling, information systems and training. K page:56 says capex is funded using existing cash and includes automation projects. Actual capex measure: reference CP-1, Financials and credit metrics / CFS Capital expenditures, K page:123.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C258",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Manufacturing and engineering require continuing reinvestment; efficiency spending competes with acquisitions and distributions for cash.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C258",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Do not equate total capex with discretionary growth spending. Maintenance/growth split and fixed/variable cost measures: Not supplied; status Not disclosed in inspected evidence.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C258",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r8",
          "label": "Working capital and cash conversion — Analyst Interpretation",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "CP-1, Direct operating cash reconciliation and bridges C2/C3, K pages 71 and 123, distinguish cash-flow asset/liability movements from management's reconciliation adjustment. K page:56 attributes lower receivables cash use partly to sales sequencing and receipt timing: “related timing of cash receipts”.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C259",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Growth, inventory needs and collection timing can absorb cash despite recognized earnings; differing movement definitions prevent a false like-for-like working-capital comparison.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C259",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Monitor cash conversion using CP-1's definitions and resolve its logged classification difference. Strict trade-working-capital cycle, DSO/DIO and contract payment terms: Not supplied here, not assumed.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C259",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r9",
          "label": "Revenue recognition — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:140: “A substantial portion of the Company's revenue is recorded at a point in time basis.” Revenue is recognized when obligations are satisfied and control transfers; limited qualifying customized contracts are recognized over time.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C260",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Most recognized sales depend on completed performance/control transfer; a minority involves progress estimation. Recognition and cash receipt are distinct.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C260",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Revenue is not a proxy for cash collection or a recurring subscription base; monitor contract assets, billings and collections if cash conversion weakens.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C260",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        },
        {
          "row_id": "r10",
          "label": "Certification and pricing-review risk — Documentary Fact",
          "cells": [
            {
              "text": "K page:10: components “are required to be certified” by authorities and often OEMs. K page:27: “Most of our U.S. Government contracts are based on a firm-fixed price”; the government may review whether pricing is “fair and reasonable” even where price is not cost-based.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C261",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Certification can impede substitution but also creates compliance and interruption risk; fixed pricing and procurement review can constrain margin recovery.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C261",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            },
            {
              "text": "Balance aftermarket durability against qualification, audit and customer-remedy risk. No specific adverse regulatory event or liability amount is inferred from risk-factor language.",
              "prov": "cited",
              "ref_id": "C261",
              "gap_ref": null,
              "unit_class": "text",
              "state": "known"
            }
          ],
          "style": "row"
        }
      ],
